## Architecture Decisions

### Decision 1: Frontend Framework

#### React with TypeScript

---

### Decision 2: Backend API Architecture

#### Separate FastAPI Service

---

### Decision 3: Client-Server Communication for Pipeline Execution

#### Option A: WebSocket for Real-Time Updates
**Description:** Persistent WebSocket connection for live updates

#### Option B: Server-Sent Events (SSE)
**Description:** One-way server-to-client event stream

**Recommendation:** **Option B (SSE)** for pipeline progress (one-way updates), with **Option A (WebSocket)** reserved for the interactive editor.

---

### Decision 4: Local vs Remote Execution Architecture

#### Split Architecture (UI Local, Pipeline Remote)
**Description:** Web UI runs locally, connects to remote pipeline server via API

| Pros | Cons |
|------|----
| Leverage powerful server hardware
| UI stays responsive during processing | Must handle file transfer |
| Can share server among team | Requires network configuration |
| Local audio playback (low latency) | More complex deployment |

---

### Decision 5: Data Persistence & Edit Storage

#### Option A: File-Based Storage (JSON/SQLite)
**Description:** Store edits in JSON files or local SQLite database

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Simple, no database server needed | Limited query capabilities |
| Easy to backup and version | Concurrent access issues |
| Human-readable (JSON) | Doesn't scale to many users |
| Works with existing file output | No built-in versioning |

#### Option B: PostgreSQL Database
**Description:** Full relational database for all data

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| ACID compliance, data integrity | Requires database server |
| Powerful querying | More complex setup |
| Excellent for audit trails | Overhead for simple use cases |
| Built-in versioning with temporal tables | Another dependency |

#### Option C: Hybrid (Files + Lightweight DB)
**Description:** Keep transcripts as JSON files, use SQLite for metadata/edits

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Transcripts stay portable | Two storage systems to manage |
| Database for edit history/undo | Sync complexity |
| Best of both approaches | May be confusing |
| Easy migration path | Extra code to maintain |


---

### Decision 6: Audio Playback & Synchronization

#### Web Audio API with Waveform Library
**Description:** Use Web Audio API with wavesurfer.js or similar

